# XSH fstat()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.fstat.001
anchor     functions/fstat.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       fstat() of a descriptor open on a regular file returns 0 and
           reports S_ISREG, the permission bits, st_size, st_nlink 1,
           st_uid the effective user ID, and the st_dev and st_ino that
           stat() reports for its name.
test       t/001-regular.c

assertion  XSH.fstat.002
anchor     functions/fstat.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       fstat() after each of several write()s on the descriptor reports
           the new file size.
test       t/002-size-follows-writes.c

assertion  XSH.fstat.003
anchor     functions/fstat.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       fstat() of a descriptor whose file has had its only name removed
           still succeeds and reports st_nlink 0.
test       t/003-unlinked.c

assertion  XSH.fstat.004
anchor     functions/fstat.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       fstat() reports S_ISFIFO for both ends of a pipe, S_ISSOCK for a
           socket and S_ISDIR for a descriptor open on a directory.
test       t/004-file-types.c

assertion  XSH.fstat.005
anchor     functions/fstat.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1988
says       fstat() of a descriptor that is not open fails with EBADF.
test       t/005-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fstat.006
anchor     functions/fstat.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       SHM
since      2001
says       fstat() of a shared memory object reports its owner (st_uid),
           its size after ftruncate() (st_size), and its read and write
           permission bits as created.
test       t/006-shm.c

assertion  XSH.fstat.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#tag_04_12
class      required
since      2008
says       After a write() on a descriptor, fstat() of it reports st_mtim
           and st_ctim later than before the write, within the system's
           time granularity (the test writes again every 10 ms for up to
           3 s until the times move).
timeout    20
test       t/007-times-updated.c
